# /etc/logrotate.d/rgt-vault
#
# Reference log rotation policy for optional file-based rgt-vault logs
# (ASVS V7 / Refs RGT-430, RGT-512).
#
# IMPORTANT DEPLOYMENT NOTES:
#   1. Default mode: file logging is NOT active. deploy/rgt-vault-go.service
#      writes only to stdout/stderr under systemd-journald (see
#      deploy/journald.conf.d/rgt-vault.conf) and has ProtectSystem=strict with
#      ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/rgt-vault, so it cannot write /var/log/rgt-vault.
#      go/internal/logging (including its FileWriter) is not yet wired into
#      `rgt-vault serve`; that wiring is follow-up work (RGT-512). Until then
#      this file is a reference policy that matches nothing on a default install.
#   2. Never send SIGHUP from postrotate. The daemon's only SIGHUP handling is
#      TLS certificate reload (ignored without --tls-cert); it does not reopen
#      log files. Rotation uses copytruncate (the writer opens with O_APPEND, so
#      truncation is safe and no reopen is needed) until SIGHUP ->
#      FileWriter.Reopen() is wired.
#   3. If you do redirect output to a file you must (a) add /var/log/rgt-vault to
#      ReadWritePaths in a drop-in and (b) make the directory writable by the
#      rgtvault user (it creates the active file), for example with
#      systemd-tmpfiles: `d /var/log/rgt-vault 0750 rgtvault adm -`.
#   4. Because rgtvault owns that directory, logrotate must NOT run as root in it:
#      a root-run rotation or a root postrotate `chown`/`chmod` on a glob there
#      follows symlinks the daemon user can plant (arbitrary-file chown/chmod).
#      This policy therefore uses `su rgtvault adm` and has no postrotate.
#   5. What this does NOT give you: tamper-evidence against rgtvault. It can
#      modify or delete its own archives. Use journald (default), off-host
#      shipping, or a root-owned archive directory fed by a root-run copy job
#      if rgtvault must not be able to alter history.

/var/log/rgt-vault/*.log {
    su rgtvault adm
    daily
    rotate 90
    maxage 90
    compress
    delaycompress
    copytruncate
    missingok
    notifempty
    # Ignored by copytruncate (the active file keeps its inode and the 0600
    # mode FileWriter gives it); kept so a later switch to the create-and-HUP
    # method (RGT-512) gets a never-world-readable file.
    create 0640 rgtvault adm
}
